"""Detailed instruction overrides for exercises with expanded guidance.

This module is intentionally additive: the existing pages manifest, scoring,
recording flow, and exercise metadata remain unchanged. These instructions
replace only the introduction-stage directions for the specified exercises.
"""

DETAILED_INSTRUCTIONS = {
    '7_Exercise_1.5_Five_Note_Scale': ['Breathe in low, expanding your stomach (diaphragmatic breath)', 'Sing do-re-mi-fa-sol-fa-mi-re-do on "ah," one note per beat, all in a single breath', 'Match each pitch to the reference tones heard', 'Hold the final "do" until the you are out of breath'],
    '8_Exercise_1.6_Staccato_vs_Legato': ['Warm up your breath: Take a low diaphragmatic breath before starting each part.', 'Part 1 - Staccato: Sing do-re-mi-fa-sol on "ah," one note per beat. For each note, use a quick, gentle pulse from your diaphragm (like a soft "huh" of air) then fully stop the airflow before the next note. There should be a brief silence between every note; if the notes bleed into each other, you’re going too fast or not releasing the air fully.', 'Keep pitch and rhythm steady in Part 1: Even though the notes are short, each one should still land clearly on pitch and last the same length - don’t let "short" turn into "rushed" or "unclear."', 'Part 2 – Legato: Sing the same five notes (do-re-mi-fa-sol) on "ah," but now keep the airflow continuous and unbroken from note to note. Think of it as one long, steady exhale that simply changes pitch as it goes -there should be no gap, no re-attack, and no audible break in the sound between notes.', 'Support the transitions in Part 2: The pitch changes should feel like sliding a dial, not flipping a switch - control this with steady diaphragm support, not by pushing more air at each new note.', 'Compare the two: Staccato = separated, pulsed, silence between notes. Legato = connected, smooth, no silence. If you record both, you should be able to see the difference as gaps (staccato) vs. one continuous waveform (legato).'],
    '9_Exercise_2.1_Major_Scale_Ascending': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath - deeper than feels necessary. Eight ascending notes use more air than you expect, and running low near the top is the most common failure point.', 'Sing the scale ascending, one note per beat: do-re-mi-fa-sol-la-ti-do: a steady, even pace, not rushed.', 'Ration your air across all eight notes: don’t spend a big, loud breath on "do" , keep volume even from note to note so you still have support left for "ti" and the top "do."', 'As pitch rises, don’t push louder or tighten your throat , the instinct is to force higher notes with more air pressure or throat tension. Instead, keep the airflow steady and let the vocal cords do the pitch change, not extra force.', 'Keep each note landing squarely on pitch before moving to the next , don’t scoop or slide up to a note; attack it directly, then move to the next step of the scale.', 'Land the final "do" (an octave up) with the same clarity as the first , if the top note is breathy, cracked, or flat, that’s a sign breath was over-spent early; slow the pace or take a bigger initial breath next time.'],
    '10_Exercise_2.2_Major_Scale_Descending': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting at the top note , you’ll need active breath support from the very first note, since descending scales are where support tends to quietly disappear.', 'Sing the scale descending, one note per beat: do-ti-la-sol-fa-mi-re-do (starting and ending on "do").', 'Understand what "sagging" actually is: as pitch drops, it’s natural to relax breath pressure along with it , this causes each note to land slightly flat and the tone to lose energy. Sagging is a breath problem, not a pitch problem.', 'Keep , or even slightly increase , breath pressure as you go down, rather than easing off. Your air support should feel like it’s working against the downward pitch, not following it.', 'Keep your posture and throat as "up" and open as they were on the top note , don’t let your body collapse or your chin drop as the pitch falls; that physical sag is often what causes the vocal sag.', 'Land the final low "do" with the same clarity and pitch accuracy as the starting note , if the ending note goes flat or breathy, that’s the sagging effect, and it means support relaxed somewhere in the middle of the descent.'],
    '11_Exercise_2.3_Minor_Scales': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting.', 'Sing the natural minor scale ascending and back down: la-ti-do-re-mi-fa-sol-la (one note per beat).', 'Notice the two half-steps that give minor its character: between ti→do and mi→fa. These are the closest, most "pulled-in" intervals in the scale , lean into how tight they feel compared to the wider whole-steps elsewhere.', 'Don’t unconsciously "correct" toward major: if you’ve trained major scales a lot, your ear may want to sharpen the 3rd note (do, here mi) or 7th (sol) upward toward a major-scale sound. Resist that pull , natural minor’s mi and sol should stay exactly where the scale places them, not bent brighter.', 'Keep breath support even across the full range , same principle as the major scale: don’t spend all your air on the first few notes, and don’t let support sag on the way back down.', 'Land the final "la" matching the pitch you started on , this checks that you held the key center steady and didn’t drift sharp or flat over the 8 notes.'],
    '12_Exercise_2.4_Intervals': ['Listen to the two notes played , a lower note, then a higher note (the size of the gap between them is the "interval" being tested).', 'Sing the lower note first, matching pitch as closely as you can, then sing the higher note, in the same order you heard them.', 'Before you leap, hear the target note in your head first , don’t jump and then search for the pitch. Briefly imagine the higher note’s pitch while still holding the lower one, then move to it directly.', 'Attack the higher note directly , don’t slide or scoop into it. A common leap mistake is sliding up through the notes in between; the jump should land cleanly on the target pitch, not glide there.', 'Keep breath support constant through the jump , a common failure is a small breath "reset" or gasp right at the leap. Keep the airflow continuous from the low note into the high note, adjusting only pitch, not air pressure.', 'Keep both notes equally clear and centered on pitch , it’s common to nail the low note but go sharp, flat, or breathy on the leap; both notes should sound equally confident.'],
    '13_Exercise_2.5_Arpeggios': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting.', 'Sing a major triad arpeggio ascending and back down: do-mi-sol-do-sol-mi-do (one note per beat).', 'Understand what causes a "break": as you leap upward through wider gaps, your voice may need to shift between chest voice (lower, heavier) and head voice (lighter, higher) to reach the top notes comfortably. A "crack" happens when that shift is sudden and uncontrolled instead of smooth.', 'Anticipate the shift before the leap, don’t fight it , as you approach the higher notes (especially the top "do"), let your voice lighten slightly rather than pushing chest-voice power all the way up. Trying to force a heavy, low-voice quality onto a high note is what usually causes the crack.', 'Keep airflow continuous through each leap , no gasping or resetting your breath between notes, even though the pitch jumps are large.', 'Match volume and tone color across all three notes (do-mi-sol) so the arpeggio sounds like one unified idea, not three disconnected pitches , the leaps should sound intentional, not like separate vocal "attempts."', 'Coming back down, keep the same smooth register transition in reverse , ease back into chest voice as you descend, rather than dropping suddenly.'],
    '14_Exercise_2.6_Pitch_Stability': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before the note starts , a shallow breath will run low before 6-8 seconds is up, and running low on air is the most common cause of both pitch drift and volume drop.', 'Listen to the reference pitch, then match and sustain it on "ah" for 6-8 seconds.', 'Set your pitch immediately, then don’t "search" for it , land on the note right away rather than sliding in from below or above; small pitch drift often starts as an uncorrected slide from the very beginning of the note.', 'Keep breath pressure constant, not decaying , the natural tendency over a long sustained note is for air pressure (and therefore pitch and volume) to gradually drop as your exhale runs out. Actively maintain , don’t ease off , your diaphragm support for the entire duration.', 'Watch the real-time feedback gauge and make small corrections early , if the gauge shows you drifting flat or sharp, make a subtle adjustment immediately rather than waiting; small early corrections are less noticeable (and easier to make) than large late ones.', 'Keep volume even by keeping airflow even , volume wavering is usually a breath-pressure symptom, not a separate issue; if you’re following step 4, volume consistency should follow naturally.', 'Finish the note as cleanly as you started it , don’t let pitch or volume sag in the final second as your breath runs low; if you consistently run out before 6-8 seconds, take a fuller breath at the start next time.'],
    '15_Exercise_3.1_Pure_Italian_Vowels': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before each round.', 'Complete 5 rounds, one per vowel: A (ah), E (eh), I (ee), O (oh), U (oo) , using their pure Italian sounds, not their English letter names.', 'Fix your mouth shape before you start the note, and don’t let it move once sustained: A = jaw dropped, open and relaxed. E = corners of the mouth slightly wide, jaw partly open. I = lips relaxed, jaw nearly closed, tongue arched forward. O = lips rounded, jaw dropped, like the shape of an "o." U = lips rounded and slightly forward, jaw mostly closed.', 'Hold each vowel for 3–4 seconds with steady airflow, keeping volume and pitch even for the full duration.', 'Watch for drift toward a diphthong , the most common error is letting a pure vowel slowly glide into a second sound (e.g., "oh" sliding toward "oh-oo", or "ay" sneaking into what should be a pure "eh"). If the vowel sound changes at all partway through the note, that’s drift , the shape should be identical at the start, middle, and end.', 'If a reference tone is available, match pitch to it first, then apply the vowel shape , don’t let matching pitch cause you to loosen the vowel shape you set in step 3.'],
    '16_Exercise_3.2_Vowel_Consistency_Ascending': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting , you’ll need to carry consistent support all the way to the top note.', 'Sing a five-note ascending scale on "ah": do-re-mi-fa-sol, one note per beat.', 'Understand why vowels drift on higher notes: as pitch rises, there’s a natural tendency to raise your jaw, tighten your tongue, or narrow your mouth shape , this often collapses a pure "ah" into a duller "uh" sound without you noticing.', 'Fix your mouth shape on the first note and don’t let it move , keep your jaw drop, tongue position, and lip shape identical from "do" through "sol." If your mouth shape doesn’t change, the vowel can’t change either.', 'Keep breath support increasing (or at least constant), not decreasing, as you ascend , a common cause of vowel drift is losing air support near the top, which makes your mouth instinctively tighten to "help." Staying well-supported removes that instinct.', 'Check the top note against the bottom note , "sol" should sound like the exact same vowel as "do," just higher in pitch. If "sol" sounds noticeably different in color/darkness from "do," that’s vowel drift.'],
    '17_Exercise_3.3_Vowel_Modification_High_Notes': ['Before starting, identify roughly where your passaggio (voice break) sits , this varies by voice type, so if you don’t know yours, try a slow slide upward on "ah" first and notice where the tone wants to shift or feels like it’s straining; that’s your approximate break point.', 'Starting a few notes below your break point, sing an ascending five-note scale on "ah" (do-re-mi-fa-sol), ending a few notes above the break.', 'As you approach the break point specifically (not before), let the "ah" open slightly toward "aw" , a small, deliberate widening of the vowel, not a full switch to a different sound.', 'Keep the tone forward and bright through the transition , don’t force the heavier, lower-voice quality to continue unchanged past the break; let it lighten as you modify the vowel, rather than pushing chest-voice power through the passaggio.', 'Keep the modification subtle , if a listener can clearly hear you switch to a different word/vowel, you’ve over-modified; the vowel should stay recognizably "ah," just slightly opened.', 'Check for a "free" tone through the break: no strain, no sudden register flip, no volume drop. If the break is still audible as a jump or crack, the modification needs to happen slightly earlier or be slightly larger.'],
    '18_Exercise_3.4_Consonant_Clarity': ['Choose a comfortable single pitch, or sing on a five-note pattern (do-re-mi-fa-sol) , one syllable per note, at a steady, moderate tempo.', 'Round 1 – "Na-Na-Na-Na-Na": Tap the tip of your tongue against the ridge just behind your upper front teeth for each "N," then release cleanly into the "ah."', 'Round 2 – "Ma-Ma-Ma-Ma-Ma": Close your lips fully for each "M," then release directly into "ah" , no lip tension carrying into the vowel.', 'Round 3 – "La-La-La-La-La": Tap the tongue tip against the same ridge as "N," but keep the sides of your tongue relaxed so air flows around it into the vowel.', 'Round 4 – "Ra-Ra-Ra-Ra-Ra": Use a light, single tap or flip of the tongue rather than a heavy American "r" , this keeps the consonant quick and prevents it from bleeding into the vowel.', 'What "crisp" means here: the consonant should happen quickly and release immediately into a fully-formed vowel , if the consonant sound lingers or the vowel starts "muddy" before settling into pure "ah," that’s a blurry attack.', 'Keep the vowel "ah" pure and identical after every consonant , the only thing that should change between rounds is the consonant; the vowel shape stays constant.', 'Aim for equal clarity and volume on every repetition , the 5th "Na" should sound as crisp as the 1st, not fatigued or softened.'],
    '19_Exercise_3.5_Tongue_Tension': ['Take a slow, relaxed diaphragmatic breath , a shallow or tense breath often causes the throat and tongue to compensate with extra effort, so starting relaxed matters here more than in most exercises.', 'Gently massage your jaw with your fingers and let it drop open loosely before making any sound.', 'Rest your tongue tip lightly against your lower front teeth , don’t grip or press, just let it rest there.', 'Choose a comfortable, low-effort pitch , not a stretch in either direction , and sustain "ah" for 5 seconds.', 'Notice if your tongue bunches up or pulls back toward your throat during the sustain , this is easiest to feel around the middle-to-back of the tongue, not the tip.', 'If the tone sounds "throaty" or swallowed, consciously relax the back of your tongue without changing your jaw position or pitch , the fix is releasing tension, not moving your mouth.', 'Repeat the same 5-second sustain on "ee" and then "oo." Note that "ee" and "oo" naturally involve a narrower mouth shape than "ah" , that’s expected. "Passive" here means no gripping or bracing in the tongue/jaw, not that the mouth shape stays identical to "ah."', 'Compare tone quality across all three vowels , if "ah" sounds free but "ee" or "oo" sounds tighter or more throaty, that vowel is holding more habitual tension and is worth repeating.'],
    '20_Exercise_3.6_Diction_Challenge': ['Phase 1 – Speak: Say the phrase "How beautiful upon the mountain" clearly and slightly slower than normal speech.', 'Exaggerate every consonant as if speaking to someone far away , over-articulate the H, B, T, F, P, M, and N sounds more than feels natural; this gives you a clear target to match later.', 'Phase 2 – Sing: Sing the same phrase on a five-note descending scale (sol-fa-mi-re-do), fitting roughly two syllables per note where needed, at a slow, steady tempo.', 'Watch for consonants dissolving into the vowel: on a spoken word, a consonant is quick and doesn’t compete with anything , on a sustained sung note, the vowel is held for the note’s full length, so there’s a strong pull to let the consonant blur or disappear (e.g., "mountain" can collapse toward "ow-uhn" with the M, T, and N all softening).', 'Keep consonants quick even though the vowel is long: articulate each consonant with the same speed and precision as in Phase 1, then let only the vowel stretch to fill the note’s duration , the consonant itself shouldn’t slow down or soften just because the note is long.', 'Pay special attention to word-ending consonants (the "l" in "beautiful," the "n" in "mountain") , these are the ones most likely to get dropped or swallowed in singing, since there’s a temptation to release the note before finishing the consonant.', 'Compare Phase 1 and Phase 2 side by side: every consonant that was crisp and audible in speech should be equally audible in the sung version , if a consonant that was clear when spoken becomes mushy or missing when sung, that’s the specific gap this exercise is meant to close.'],
    '21_Exercise_4.1_Connecting_Notes': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting.', 'Sing three connected notes, do-re-mi, on "ah," one note per beat, all in a single breath.', 'Change pitch without closing your throat between notes. A glottal stop happens when your vocal cords briefly pinch shut to "re-attack" the next note , instead, let the pitch change happen while the airflow and vocal cords stay continuously engaged, the same way a slide changes pitch without ever stopping.', 'Keep the airflow constant across all three notes , don’t add a small breath pulse or pause at each new pitch; the air should feel like one unbroken stream from the first note to the last.', 'Keep the vowel and volume identical on all three notes , the only thing that should change from note to note is pitch.', 'Check for a "seam": if you can hear a click, a small gap, or a re-attack at either transition (do→re or re→mi), that’s a glottal stop , slow the exercise down and focus on that specific transition until it disappears.'],
    '22_Exercise_4.2_Legato_Scales': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting , you’ll use it evenly across all five notes, so don’t spend it unevenly at the start.', 'Sing the melody do-re-mi-re-do on "ah," one note per beat, keeping the same continuous-airflow, no-glottal-stop connection you used in Connecting Notes , this is the same skill, extended to five notes with a shape layered on top.', 'Shape the phrase as a gentle volume arc: very slightly swell in volume as you rise toward "mi" (the peak), then very slightly recede as you fall back to "do." This is a subtle dynamic shape, not a big or sudden change.', 'Avoid "bumps" at each note change: a bump usually comes from a small, unconscious extra push of breath every time the pitch changes. Keep your breath pressure smooth and continuous , let pitch and the gentle volume arc be the only things moving, not a pulse of air at each syllable.', 'Check that it sounds like one sustained idea: if you can hear five separate "attempts" instead of one shape that happens to change pitch five times, slow down and focus on removing the breath pulse at each transition.'],
    '23_Exercise_4.3_Phrase_Shaping': ['Look at the 4-bar melody before singing it. Bars 1-2 form the first half of the musical thought; bars 3-4 complete it , like a sentence with a comma in the middle and a period at the end.', 'Before you sing, plan your breath: take one full, generous breath and try to sing all 4 bars in a single breath. Gauge honestly whether your air will realistically last , don’t just start and hope.', 'If you genuinely can’t make it in one breath, take exactly one breath, at the end of bar 2 , the natural boundary, like a comma , not before.', 'Never break mid-bar: a breath in the middle of bar 1, 2, 3, or 4 interrupts a musical idea before it’s finished, the same way pausing mid-word would in speech.', 'Treat the phrase like a sentence: bars 1-2 are one clause, bars 3-4 complete the thought. You wouldn’t pause randomly in the middle of a spoken sentence , plan your breath the same way, only at the natural pause point.', 'If you ran out of air before bar 2 ended, that’s useful information: take a fuller breath next attempt, or check if you’re over-spending air (too loud, too much breath pressure) early in the phrase. NOTE: Breathing a “fuller breath” does NOT mean you must puff up the chest and raise the shoulders. Keep your posture grounded and place your arms on your sides where the ribs are. If you can feel the ribs expanding as the lungs push them outwards, you’re doing the right thing.', '| Bar | Beat 1            | Beat 2            | Beat 3                                | Beat 4           |', '| --- | ----------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------- |', '| 1   | do (C4), quarter  | re (D4), quarter  | mi (E4), quarter                      | fa (F4), quarter |', '| 2   | sol (G4), quarter | la (A4), quarter  | sol (G4), half (holds through beat 4) |                  |', '| 3   | fa (F4), quarter  | mi (E4), quarter  | re (D4), quarter                      | mi (E4), quarter |', '| 4   | fa (F4), quarter  | re (D4), quarter  | do (C4), half (holds through beat 4)  |                  |'],
    '24_Exercise_4.4_Melodic_Etude': ['Take a full diaphragmatic breath before starting. This étude has two 4-bar phrases, so plan your air for each phrase individually rather than trying to stretch one breath across all 8 bars.', 'Sing bars 1–4 on "ah," in one breath: do-re-mi-fa / sol-la-ti-do / ti-la-sol-fa / mi-re-do (held). This phrase rises to its peak at bar 2 (high "do"), then descends and resolves on a held "do" at the end of bar 4 , that held note is your breath point.', 'Breathe at the end of bar 4, not before. Even if you feel like you have air to spare mid-phrase, hold the line until that resolution point , breaking early undercuts the arc.', 'Sing bars 5–8 on "ah," in a second breath: mi-fa-sol-la / ti-do-re-do / ti-la-sol-fa / mi-re-do (held). This phrase mirrors the shape of the first, but reaches higher at bar 6 (up to "re," a step above the first phrase’s peak) , this is the étude’s climax before it settles back down to resolve on the final held "do" at bar 8.', 'Connect every note with continuous airflow , no glottal stops, no breath pulses at each pitch change. As in earlier legato exercises, changing pitch should never involve momentarily closing or re-attacking; the air stream stays constant while only pitch moves.', 'Keep the vowel "ah" identical on every note, high or low. Don’t let the vowel narrow, darken, or shift as pitch rises toward bar 2 or bar 6 , the vowel shape is one of the few things that should not change across the whole étude.', 'Shape each phrase as an arc, not a flat line: let your volume swell gently as you climb toward each phrase’s peak (bar 2 in phrase one, bar 6 in phrase two), then ease back down as you descend to the resolution. Keep the swell subtle , this is a shape, not a shout.', 'Match your starting and ending energy. The final note of bar 8 should sound as full and supported as the very first note of bar 1 , don’t let volume, breath support, or pitch accuracy fade as the étude winds down. A trailing-off ending usually means the breath in bars 5–8 was spent too early on the climb to bar 6.', 'Check the two phrases against each other: they should sound like variations on the same musical idea , same shape, same vowel, same energy , with phrase two simply reaching a bit higher before resolving.'],
    '24b_Exercise_4.5_Simple_Vocalises': ['Choose your vowel: "ah" or "oo." Both work the same way here, pick whichever you want to focus on today.', 'Take one full diaphragmatic breath before starting, this entire vocalise is sung in a single breath, so plan your air for all five notes up front.', 'Sing the five-note pattern do-mi-sol-mi-do, one note per beat, holding the final "do" for two beats.', 'Connect every note with continuous airflow, including the two skips (do→mi, mi→sol), unlike a stepwise scale, these are small leaps rather than adjacent steps, so there’s a stronger pull to "re-attack" each note. Resist that: keep the same unbroken air stream you’d use on a stepwise line, even across the jump.', 'Shape the phrase as a gentle rise and release: let volume swell slightly as you climb from "do" to the peak at "sol," then ease back down through "mi" to the closing "do." Keep the shape subtle, not a big dynamic swing.', 'Keep your chosen vowel and pitch equally clear on all five notes, the "ah" or "oo" shouldn’t shift in color or the airflow waver, whether you’re on the low "do" or the peak "sol."', 'Take your one planned breath before starting, and don’t break the line until the final "do" has fully finished, this is a single continuous phrase from first note to last.'],
}


def get_detailed_instructions(exercise_id):
    """Return detailed instructions for an exercise, or None if unchanged."""
    return DETAILED_INSTRUCTIONS.get(exercise_id)
